# 3. Log Signature Matching — Corrected and Optimized Pseudocode

## A. Corrected Algorithm: Brute-Force String Matching

**Input:** Log string and signature pattern.

**Output:** All starting positions where the pattern occurs.

```text
ALGORITHM CORRECTED_FIND_SIGNATURE(log, pattern)

    IF pattern is empty
        RETURN empty list
    END IF

    positions ← empty list

    FOR i ← 0 TO LENGTH(log) - LENGTH(pattern)
        IF log[i : i + LENGTH(pattern)] = pattern
            ADD i TO positions
        END IF
    END FOR

    RETURN positions

END ALGORITHM
```

## B. Optimized Algorithm: Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP)

```text
ALGORITHM OPTIMIZED_FIND_SIGNATURE(log, pattern)

    IF pattern is empty
        RETURN empty list
    END IF

    BUILD the LPS array for pattern

    positions ← empty list
    i ← 0
    j ← 0

    WHILE i < LENGTH(log)

        IF log[i] = pattern[j]
            i ← i + 1
            j ← j + 1
        END IF

        IF j = LENGTH(pattern)
            ADD (i - j) TO positions
            j ← LPS[j - 1]

        ELSE IF i < LENGTH(log) AND log[i] ≠ pattern[j]

            IF j > 0
                j ← LPS[j - 1]
            ELSE
                i ← i + 1
            END IF

        END IF

    END WHILE

    RETURN positions

END ALGORITHM
```

**Time Complexity:**
- Corrected brute-force matching: O(nm) worst case.
- Optimized KMP matching: O(n + m).

Here, n is the log length and m is the pattern length. LPS means Longest Proper Prefix which is also a Suffix.

**Purpose:** Find all occurrences of a suspicious signature in a log, including occurrences at the final possible position and overlapping matches.
